1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,724
This is an OpenFOAM case — the damBreak tutorial — open in FoDE. The dictionary is a tree on the left; on the right, the mesh it describes, and the water column setFieldsDict will fill — drawn as soon as the case opened.

2
00:00:04,724 --> 00:00:09,800
Open setFieldsDict: it is the file that puts that water there.

3
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:14,199
Select the box that defines it, and the editor below jumps to the line it came from.

4
00:00:14,199 --> 00:00:18,682
Change the water column — make it wider and taller.

5
00:00:18,682 --> 00:00:23,873
The overlay follows the edit. No re-run, no export, no guessing at coordinates.

6
00:00:23,873 --> 00:00:28,885
Save the file.

7
00:00:28,885 --> 00:00:33,884
The mesh has to exist before the fields can be set, so run blockMesh.

8
00:00:33,884 --> 00:00:44,484
It runs in the Terminal tab, in the case directory, and tees its output to a log.

9
00:00:44,484 --> 00:00:53,401
Now setFields. The dialog offers the flags rather than asking you to remember them, and shows the exact command it will send. setFields rewrites 0/ in place, so it offers to restore 0/ from 0.orig first — checked by default, and visible in the command.

10
00:00:53,401 --> 00:01:01,484
Run it.

11
00:01:01,484 --> 00:01:08,417
And the solver. Run Solver reads interFoam from controlDict and shows the command; the parallel option waits for a decomposed case.

12
00:01:08,417 --> 00:01:22,484
interFoam runs to the end time, and the time directories it writes land in the file list as they appear.

13
00:01:22,484 --> 00:01:26,484
Then the logs the runs left behind are condensed to a report: what ran, how far it got, and every warning it printed.
